Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt76-usb: TX coalescing - batch kick + USB hold

Task-45 forensics on mt7921u AP mode showed aggregation effectively
never engages below USB-saturation rates: 95% of PPDUs carried 1-2
MPDUs, AP->client cost 3.3x the airtime of client->AP for identical
traffic, and every trickle frame wakes the weak-link client radio once.

Two host-side fixes:

1. mt76_txq_schedule_pending_wcid() kicked the hardware queue inside
   the per-frame dequeue loop - each buffered frame became its own
   immediate USB submission. Kick once per touched queue after the
   loop instead (batch-kick; always on).

2. Optional AC-queue coalescing hold (soft hrtimer, module param
   sl30_tx_hold_us, 0 = off): kicks on AC queues arm the timer; its
   softirq callback submits all four AC queues as one batch. The
   PSD/mc pipe is never held. stop/deinit paths flush the hold so no
   frame leaks.

Task-46 A/B verdict (2026-09-07, live on the bench AP):
- hold=0 + bulk windowed TCP: full aggregation already engages
  (250 Mbps, PPDUs up to 29-37 MPDU deep, ~97% of bytes aggregated).
- hold=2000: -28% bulk TCP throughput; hold=8000: -50%.
- reactive/lock-step trickle (the Mac's flow shape) never aggregates
  at any hold - the window only adds its own latency (RTT 0.9 ->
  2.8 -> 9.0 ms for 0/2/8 ms holds).
=> the hold ships OFF by default (sl0030b); it remains a documented
experiment knob. The aggregation starvation for trickle traffic is
a queue-depth problem the host cannot fix without paying the full
latency, not a missing coalescing mechanism.

